            <div xml:id="d429">

                <pb facs="0664" n="562" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg562"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.90H/38: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Straus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-18T12:00:00+01:00">October 18,
                            1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 18—11:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>905. Department’s 425, October 14, 8 p.m. The Embassy found the text of article 4
                    as suggested in the Embassy’s telegram No. 548, June 29, 11 a.m., was approved
                    by the Department’s telegram No. 263, July 3, 6 p.m. The present proposed Afghan
                    alteration would make that article read as follows: <pb facs="0665" n="563"
                        xml:id="pg563"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“With respect to customs duties or charges of any kind imposed on or in
                            connection with importation or exportation, and with respect to the
                            method of levying such duties or charges, and with respect to all rules
                            and formalities in connection with importation or exportation, and with
                            respect to all laws or regulations affecting the sale or use of imported
                            goods within the country, any advantage, favor, privilege or immunity
                            which has been or may hereafter be granted by the United States of
                            America or the Kingdom of Afghanistan to any article originating in or
                            destined for any third country shall be according [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">accorded</hi>] to the like article originating
                            in or destined for the Kingdom of Afghanistan or the United States of
                            America, respectively.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>As pointed out in previous telegrams Afghan Legation understands the principle
                    enunciated in the article is that of most-favored-nation treatment. If the
                    language quoted above is satisfactory the signature of the treaty could be
                    arranged in short order. On the other hand, if the Department wishes to submit
                    other new language, matter must wait further references to Kabul.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Straus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
